{"name":"North Star","hex":"#f2dea4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"North Star","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The current northern pole star, the star nearest the northern celestial pole, which for Earth has been Polaris for the last 1500 years.","A locality in the Gwydir council area, north eastern New South Wales, Australia."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An aim or a goal, a dependable guiding light in a quickly changing world."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/North_Star"}],"entries":[{"title":"North Star (Star Trek: Enterprise)","blocks":[{"text":"\"North Star\" is the 61st episode of the American science fiction television series Star Trek: Enterprise, the ninth episode of the third season. It first aired on November 12, 2003, on UPN. It was written by David A. Goodman and directed by David Straiton."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Star_(Star_Trek%3A_Enterprise)","wikidata":"Q2996346"},{"title":"Polaris","blocks":[{"text":"Polaris is a yellow supergiant star in the northern circumpolar constellation of Ursa Minor. It is designated α Ursae Minoris and is commonly called the North Star. With an apparent magnitude that fluctuates around 1.98, it is the brightest star in the constellation and is readily visible to the naked eye at night. The position of the star lies less than 1° away from the north celestial pole, making it the current northern pole star. The stable position of the star in the Northern Sky makes it useful for navigation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Ursa_Minor_constellation_map.svg/330px-Ursa_Minor_constellation_map.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polaris","wikidata":"Q12980"},{"title":"North","blocks":[{"text":"North is one of the four compass points or cardinal directions. It is the opposite of south and is perpendicular to east and west. North is a noun, adjective, or adverb indicating direction or geography."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Compass_Rose_English_North.svg/330px-Compass_Rose_English_North.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North","wikidata":"Q659"},{"title":"Star","blocks":[{"text":"A star is a luminous spheroid of plasma held together by self-gravity. The nearest star to Earth is the Sun. Many other stars are visible to the naked eye at night; their immense distances from Earth make them appear as fixed points of light. The most prominent stars have been categorised into constellations and asterisms, and many of the brightest stars have proper names. Astronomers have assembled star catalogues that identify the known stars and provide standardized stellar designations. The observable universe contains an estimated 1022 to 1024 stars. Only about 4,000 of these stars are visible to the naked eye—all within the Milky Way galaxy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star","wikidata":"Q523"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/500px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/1280px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/500px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Polaris components as seen by the Hubble Space Telescope","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/AlphaUMiLightCurve.png/500px-AlphaUMiLightCurve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/AlphaUMiLightCurve.png/1280px-AlphaUMiLightCurve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/AlphaUMiLightCurve.png/500px-AlphaUMiLightCurve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A light curve for Polaris, plotted from TESS data[29]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlphaUMiLightCurve.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Polaris_time-lapse_illustrating_Cepheid_type_variability.gif/500px-Polaris_time-lapse_illustrating_Cepheid_type_variability.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Polaris_time-lapse_illustrating_Cepheid_type_variability.gif/500px-Polaris_time-lapse_illustrating_Cepheid_type_variability.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 4-day time lapse of Polaris illustrating its Cepheid type variability","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolaris_time-lapse_illustrating_Cepheid_type_variability.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Polaris_surface_image.png/500px-Polaris_surface_image.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Polaris_surface_image.png/500px-Polaris_surface_image.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Polaris mapped by the CHARA Array","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolaris_surface_image.png"},{"srcset